    slug: "creature-collecting",
    title: "Your Yakkamon",
    desc: "Around 50\u201360 species at launch \u2014 and four separate things decide whether an individual monster is worth keeping.",
    detail: "The team has said to expect roughly <strong>50\u201360 Yakkamon at launch</strong>, with more arriving each chapter rather than all at once. Four things make one individual worth having. <strong>Its species</strong> decides what it can gather at all (see <a href=\"?system=type-locking\">Type Locking</a>) and also fixes its rarity tier. <strong>Its stats</strong> roll differently on every individual, so two of the same species won't be identical \u2014 it's worth hunting the same creature again to chase a better roll. <strong>Its traits</strong> are randomly rolled percentage-chance bonuses that change how it farms. <strong>Its skills</strong> decide what it does in combat.<br><br>Underneath sits more machinery than the marketing suggests: real genetics, species-specific stat curves, <strong>hidden initial values you can't see when a monster spawns</strong>, and training values that shift stats depending on how you raise it. That last part matters \u2014 a species that's naturally defensive but rolled a high genetic attack stat can be trained into an attacker, which is where individual build expression comes from.<br><br>One idea the team explored and dropped: <strong>age and weight affecting stats</strong>. Bryn called it a step too complicated and backed away from it.<br><br>Crucially, your monsters are also your boost items. Rather than crafting a statue and placing it on your land the way Sunflower Land does, <strong>the buffs live on the creatures themselves</strong> \u2014 gathering multipliers, yield and speed bonuses. The team you farm with is the source of your bonuses, not a decoration you buy. The free-mint stream gave that a name: every monster carries a <a href=\"?system=utilities-auras\">utility</a>, named on Legendaries and randomly rolled on everything else.<br><br><strong>Thirteen types at launch, one per monster.</strong> Bryn confirmed the type list is 13 to start \u2014 fire, water, rock and the other familiar creature-collector elements, &ldquo;with our own spin&rdquo; \u2014 and that <strong>every Yakkamon has exactly one type at release</strong>. Dual types are &ldquo;definitely coming&rdquo;, later. For <a href=\"?system=type-locking\">type locking</a> that means thirteen buckets of resources and a monster can only ever open one of them.<br><br><strong>No wearable gear.</strong> Asked about equipment and stat boosts, Bryn was flat: &ldquo;We&rsquo;re not doing wearable gear for this game. The Yakkamon have the stats.&rdquo; There may be a single <strong>held item</strong> in the Pok&eacute;mon mold \u2014 one thing granting one passive \u2014 but no clothes and no equipment slots. Stats are baked in at generation from genetics and training values, and improve through time invested, training and, later, breeding. If you want a stronger monster, you raise one or you find one; you don&rsquo;t dress one. <strong>Leveling</strong> comes from combat, training and food \u2014 &ldquo;all of the above&rdquo;, with more systems to come.",
