    desc: "Plots wear out as you farm them, and refertilizing costs resources \u2014 a sink that doesn't exist in Sunflower Land.",
    detail: "Revealed on August 21 from Spencer's job-card screenshots, and one of the more consequential mechanics announced so far.<br><br><strong>Farm plots degrade with use.</strong> The job card carries a degradation marker \u2014 the example shown read <strong>degraded 1.6\u00d7</strong>, meaning that plot now farms more slowly until you refertilize it. Making fertilizer <strong>costs resources</strong>, so upkeep is a genuine, permanent sink on your production rather than a cosmetic status.<br><br><strong>Degradation comes from use, not from absence.</strong> That distinction was settled on the free-mint stream, and it corrects the way we first read this system. Asked what happens if a player is away for a week or a month, Bryn said: <strong>&ldquo;It just pauses the progress. There is no punitive system for not logging in.&rdquo;</strong> Your Yakkamon work until the bins and job areas are full, then stop and wait. &ldquo;There isn&rsquo;t any decay. There is no neglect feature or anything like that&rdquo; \u2014 with a hundred monsters on the farm, an attention system for each one would be too punishing. So a plot wears out because you farmed it, and a full bin is production you didn&rsquo;t bank, but a farm you walk away from is a farm on pause. Bryn was equally explicit that the intent is <em>not</em> to punish players for going wide \u2014 upkeep is meant to be manageable.<br><br><strong>Plot groups are non-linear, and that's deliberate.</strong> Growing tiles come in groups \u2014 three in the screenshot shown. Your first plot is free and requires upkeep; the second and third also require upkeep, but <strong>the upkeep cost scales more slowly than the production does</strong>. Filling out a group is therefore strictly better value per unit of upkeep than spreading thin, which is the team's way of pushing you to invest early and deep rather than wide and shallow.<br><br>No numbers beyond the 1.6\u00d7 example have been published \u2014 not the degradation rate, not the fertilizer recipe, not the group size in the live build.",
